---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base
  title="Dev / Scan Process"
  description="How Tyler Walker develops, camera-scans, converts, and prepares film photographs for the web."
>
  <header class="page-head process-head">
    <h1>Dev / Scan Process</h1>
    <p>
      This is my typical workflow. Individual rolls may use a different lab,
      chemistry, or scanning approach; when that context matters, I'll note it
      alongside the roll.
    </p>
    <p>
      I've started developing myself but I've also used:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Moody's Film Lab in Burien, WA</li>
      <li>We Develop Film in Edmonds, WA</li>
      <li>Panda Lab in Seattle, WA</li>
      <li>The Darkroom</li>
      <li>Color House in New York, NY</li>
    </ul>
  </header>

  <ol class="process-steps">
    <li class="process-step">
      <span class="process-step__number" aria-hidden="true">01</span>
      <div>
        <h2>Development</h2>
        <p>
         I have been developing most of my own film at Tacoma Photo Center using the chemicals they have available.
         If you're interested in developing yourself it's only about $10/hr and all materials are provided!
        </p>
        <p>
          At home, I've got a setup that I am starting to use where I use CineStil checmicals and a patterson tank.
          Once the chemicals are done and the film is dry I scan using my Valoi Easy120.
        </p>
      </div>
    </li>

    <li class="process-step">
      <span class="process-step__number" aria-hidden="true">02</span>
      <div>
        <h2>Negative Preparation</h2>
        <p>
          Before scanning, I use an Ilford anti-static cloth to get all of the dust and particles off.
        </p>
      </div>
    </li>

    <li class="process-step">
      <span class="process-step__number" aria-hidden="true">03</span>
      <div>
        <h2>Camera Scanning</h2>
        <p>
          I use my Nikon Zf with the Valoi Easy120 to capture both my 120 and 35mm film. The steps I follow are:
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li>Adjust the exposure so that the film border is bright, but not clipping.</li>
          <li>Zoom in, focus on the grain.</li>
          <li>Using the NX Tether app, capture each frame and move to adjust.</li>
        </ol>
      </div>
    </li>

    <li class="process-step">
      <span class="process-step__number" aria-hidden="true">04</span>
      <div>
        <h2>Conversion</h2>
        <p>
          RAW captures are brought into Adobe Lightroom Classic and converted
          with Negative Lab Pro. I establish a neutral starting point from the
          film border, crop the frame, and let the conversion preserve as much
          useful highlight and shadow information as possible.
        </p>
        <p>
          On occassion I will also use the Film Lab app for speed, but the results vary for me.
        </p>
      </div>
    </li>

    <li class="process-step">
      <span class="process-step__number" aria-hidden="true">05</span>
      <div>
        <h2>Edit &amp; Cleanup</h2>
        <p>
          I will export .tiff files from Lightroom Classic or the Film Lab app and import those images into Lightroom for editing.
        </p>
        <p>
          Cleanup is generally limited to dust and physical defects from the
          scanning process.
        </p>
      </div>
    </li>

    <li class="process-step">
      <span class="process-step__number" aria-hidden="true">06</span>
      <div>
        <h2>Web Output</h2>
        <p>
          Finished images are exported as high-quality JPEGs for posting on Instagram or other social sites.
        </p>
      </div>
    </li>
  </ol>

  <aside class="process-note">
    <h2>Why describe the process?</h2>
    <p>
      Film does not remove editing; it moves those decisions into development,
      printing, scanning, and conversion. Sharing the process makes those choices
      visible and gives each roll a little more context.
    </p>
  </aside>
</Base>
